{% extends "voc2_en.j2" %}

{% block summary %}
<p>OSLO Organization adds terms to three existing vocabularies that describe organizations.</p><p>The first vocabulary is <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-org/#org:Organization">W3C Organization Ontology</a>, which in turn is an extension <a href="http://xmlns.com/foaf/spec/#term_Organization">of Organization of FOAF.</a> It adds entities such as Organizational Unit, Formal Organization, and Partnership. Branches can also be described and attention is paid to events such as mergers of organizations. W3C also further developed the term Membership of FOAF and a Position entity was provided to describe the function that a person holds within an organization.</p><p>A further specialization from Formal Organization to Registered Organization is based on the <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-regorg/">W3C Registered Organization Vocabulary</a>. This adds terms to describe officially registered organizations, e.g. by means of their registration number and legal name.</p><p>Finally, the <a href="https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/asset/cpov/home">ISA Core Public Organisation vocabulary</a> was examined. This vocabulary is important for the <a href="https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/asset/core_public_service/description">ISA Core Public Service vocabulary</a> because public service is defined by the public organization responsible for setting it up. Some characteristics of public organisations in this vocabulary were elevated by ISA to the organisational level (e.g. preferred name, purpose, classification).</p><p>What does OSLO-Organization add to these three vocabularies?</p><p>Subclasses are provided to specify the changes that organizations undergo during their life cycle, i.e. incorporation, merger, demerger, discontinuance and replacement. Establishment was already provided by ISA Core Public Organisation in order to be able to designate an organisation as public on the basis of the legal framework in which it was established.</p><p>OSLO Organization also adds the Capacity entity. This is a position within an organisation held by a person who, for example, realises a certain public service (e.g. diversity officer x at organisation y realises a diversity plan z). In other words, Capacity is both a Position and an Agent and is therefore a subclass of both entities.</p><p>Another addition relates to the entity Establishment. OSLO Organisation offers the possibility to describe which site objects with an address (addressable objects) make up the branch of an organisation. This is important for officially registered organizations where the official address of the branches is kept and not just a contact address.</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block status %}<p>A list of the most recent vocabularies can be found at
<a href="https://data.vlaanderen.be/ns">https://data.vlaanderen.be/ns</a>.</p><br />

<p>This document is the specification of a vocabulary and provides a shared conceptual framework for certain concepts.</p>
<p>On 04/10/2018, this document was published as a Standard by the OSLO Data Standards working group, ratified by the Flemish Information and ICT Policy Steering Body on 03/07/2018.
This standard has the status of 'voluntary application'.</p>

<p>Feedback on this specification can be given as a topic in our <a href="https://github.com/Informatievlaanderen/OSLO-Public-Discussion">public review discussion list</a>.</p>
{% endblock %}
